<> Monom 0.1, a programming language, originally designed by Pablo de La Fuente Sancho, 2025


<> This is a single line comment
</
    This is a multiline comment.
    </
        It can be nested.
    />
/>

::: This is a single line doc comment.
:/
    This is a multiline doc comment.
    :/
        It can be nested.
    /:

    The next doc section will end the effect of the documentation above, since it affects the next code,
    unless docs are placed before, like with Doc(file). You can use Doc(nothing) for the purpose of ignoring the previous docs,
    or to write not relevant documentation. Anyway, docs should be always to document some code.
/:

::: Doc(file) This is a Monom file (.mnm) showing the syntax of the 0.1 version of the language.


<> this is a generalized annotation (@[< >] instead of @[ ]), affects the next code until the opposite is written (not every annotation supports this)
@[<allow.prefix_before_name(false)>]


::: Entry point for an executable
main() = (
    </
        Monom is a high-to-low level language, combining functional, OOP and actors paradigms, along other features explained in this sample code.
        The syntax is designed to be robust (not dependent on indent), intuitive (tries to resemble simple mathematics, and
        to develop as much as possible from a little basis), comfortable (uses EOL (equivalent to ; in the syntax) as the separator between expressions,
        but a lot of them can be splitted in several lines) and easy to read. Minimalism is also welcome, but readability and coherence, helpful
        both for humans and for AI, is more important. The syntax is based mainly on expressions, something that will be understood later on. The aim
        of that is to resemble mathematics and to forget about syntax when writing, since symbols is the only thing that matters.
    />


    <> declarations (constant by default)

    d1 = 1
    d2: Int32 = 2
    def d3: Int64 <> definition
        </
            Takes the default value until assigned, for integers it is -1, for naturals 1, for floats -0.125,
            and for a new type, you can override __defaultValue__. The values are chosen so that they represent the type,
            so -1 for integers to differentiate them from naturals (1), aka unsigned ints in other languages, and
            -0.125 for floats to show it has decimal digits and that it can be negative. The default value is not 0
            to be able to tell the difference. The maximum, minimum and these other things are atributes from the type, like
            Nat64.maxValue or Flot32.minValueOverZero from typical numbers. You also have, for instance, in Float32,
            Float32.floatNaN, and the methods .is_NaN() and .is_a_number(), to manage NaN (not a number).
        />
    d3 = 3 <> then, we can assign the value for the defined identifier (the definition can be understood as some sort of interface of the type)

    ::: Let's create an alias so that we don't need to write so much. This text is its documentation.
    use Int = Int64

    ::: another alias, to unlink the function from the namespace so that we write less
    use std.io.stdOut.print <> the last field is the introduced alias, equivalent to use print = std.io.stdOut.print

    @[<allow.prefix_before_name(true)>]

    <> prefixes can be either before or in the type of an identifier, although in the type is better
    <> const is not needed, since it is the default, but we will use it for the examples
    <> local makes the identifier local bounded, therefore unreachable from an inner scope (private, the default, allows frowing down)
    <> local things can not be used for closures

    ::: the prefix is before the data type
    d4: const Int = 4

    ::: no type is required to write the prefix
    d5: local = 5

    ::: although not recommended (and corrected by the formatter, mnmfmt), you can write the prefix
    ::: as a prefix to the identifier instead of as a prefix to the type
    local const d6 = 6

    ::: a mix of them in different positions is allowed too
    const d7: local = 7

    ::: the type, as expected, is written the same way always, with or without prefixes
    local d8: local Int = 8


    <> functions: all of them have just one argument (unary), and are applied by a blank space like f x

    ::: 0 arguments
    f1() = print "this is f1"
    </
        also valid as:
        f1 none = print "this is f1"
        f1(none) = print "this is f1"
    />

    ::: 1 argument
    f2 x = x + 1

    _ = f2 1 + 3 </ Because of the precedence, this expression is equivalent to (f2 1) + 3
        since if you see f2(1) + 3 you would expect that the function is grouped like (f2 1) + 3 and NOT like f2 ((1) + 3).
        If you prefer, you can use parentheses, it means the same, just applied to parentheses that return x />

    f3(x) = x * 321 <> the parentheses have no effect here

    nothing <> type Nothing (with value nothing), which can be ignored

    std.io.stdOut.println "Hello World!" <> returns nothing, so the result can be ignored

    <> to ignore something that is not nothing, i.e. something (simply called thing, from Thing), the do word is used
    do f1()
    do f3(5) <> you can call with parentheses

    ::: in functions, the body ends with ; (EOL), but if a declaration, a definition, use, import, export or a do statement follows, the next expression is included
    f4(x) = <> since the equal expects a expression and it is not complete, EOL is skipped
        use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println <> a use expression to unlink readln and println from their namespace, shortening the path; EOL skipped

        ; <> equivalent to EOL, it is not skipped, just ; ; = ;, so it is considered the same as the last (skipped) EOL
        <> the same with empty lines

        do f1() <> a do statement, EOL skipped
        do println("You can call with parentheses, but it is not mandatory.") </ here, do is not needed (println returns nothing),
            but in order to continue the body of the function so that it does not end here, we will use do (a do statement can never
            be the last expression of a function)
            />

        def a: Int <> a declaration (or definition, in this case), EOL is skipped again
        a = readln().get_length() <> again, another declaration
        a + <> the + waits to be complete
        b
        + x <> this + can only be complete if it follows the previous expression, so for unary - use (-x) when written at the beginning of a line

    ::: f4 could also be written like this...
        f5 x = use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println;use{var b};;; do f1()
                do println("You can call with parentheses, but it is not mandatory."); def a: Int
    a = readln().length(); a +; b ; + x

    <> ...but do not do it please! If the body of the function gets long, it is better to wrap it in parentheses, like with this main function

    ::: the formatted function would look like this:
    f4(x) =
        use std.io.stdOut .readln, .println

        do f1()
        do println("You can call with parentheses, but it is not mandatory.")

        def a: Int
        a = readln().get_length()
        a + b + x


    ::: for more than one argument, functions can take tuples
    f6(x, y) = x + y

    ::: a lambda function
    lam1 = fun x = x + 1

    ::: formatted
    lam2 =
        fun x = x + 1

    ::: another one
    def lam3: Int -> Int
    lam3 = (a => a * 5) <> called soft lambdas (the reason will be understood later), take just one arg, and it is recommended to wrap them in parenthesis

    ::: another soft lambda
    lam4 =
        x => -21 * x

    ::: a real/hard lambda with auto arguments in a tuple ($1 is the first, $2 the second...)
    lam5 =
        fun = <> auto arguments of a tuple, if you want a 0 arguments lambda, use "fun() ="
            $0 + $1 <> using the pipe, which has low precedence so that you can connect code with operators, like here (passing the result of +)
            |> fun = $$0 + 234 <> every nested auto-args lambda gains one $ in its auto arguments

    ::: Multiline can be done like this too, with single line doc after other.
    ::: Hard lambdas can call themselves lambda, but only the inner one (soft ones can not be recursive at all, that is why they can be called soft).
    ::: Here we can see how auto expression completion is helpful to write minimalist functions (remember indentation does not matter!)
    factorial n =
        (1, n) |> <> again, this expects an expression, so it skips the EOL, like above |> at the beginning linked to the previous expression
        fun (acc, num) =
            match num
            to 1 => acc </ ends with EOL, except if a to expression is next, in which case the code between the arrow and the next  to
                will behave like if it were within parentheses (a block), we will see how to tackle the problem of nested match expressions later />
            to else =>
                lambda(acc * num, num - 1)

    use std.io.stdOut.println

    <> nested match, how to differentiate whether a to belongs to one or another? by default, to the deepest one
    nested_match(x: Int) =
        match x
        to Int<12 => "less than 12"
        to if x >= 12 && <= 66 => <> * see the comment after this function
            match x ( <> if parenthesis and to is found (no matter the space or EOLs between), the parentheses delimit the body of the match expression
            to in 12, 13, 14, 15, ...[6, 7, 8, 9].map(n => n + 10) => "in 12-19, 'to in' calls the .has method of the object received,"
                <+> "otherwise we would have matched to a tuple or an array"
            to if [20..:(10)101].map(d => ...([0..==9].map n => d + n)).has x => "20-100"
            )
        to if > 66 => "greater than 66" <> again, a to branch in the outer match, see **

    </
        *:
        && followed by >, >=, <, <=, ==, !=, ~== or ¬ takes the first element of the previous expression (with the priority level of &&) to the middle,
        so x < y && > z is understood as (x < y) && (x > z), it is just syntactic sugar
        **:
        The use of >, >=, <, <=, ==, !=, or ~== as prefix compares with the object of the match, so that you do not need to write a lot,
        so match x to if > k => is like match x to if x > k
    />

    :/
        an example of lambda in which we want to call the outer lambda
        notice how we get rid of unnecessary parentheses: the body seems delimited by space like in Python, but it is not (safer against copy-paste errors)
    /:
    collatz number : Int -> Nat64 =
        is_even n = n % 2 == 0 ; is_odd(n) =
                                    n % 2 == 1 <> thanks to the syntax, we can indent like if we were writing in a paper

        ::: a bridge value
        num =
            if ([1, 2, 4, -1, -2, -4].has number) return 0 <> early return from the function
            else
                number </
                        the result of the if expression as a whole is passed to the pipe,
                        although return is an early return to avoid the strictness of the last expression,
                        so, that branch, is known to scape the pipe (returns from the function, i.e. collatz)
                    />
            |> __.abs() <> we refer in a pipe to the value passed through as __, here we take the abs using a function/method from Int (Int64) and assign it to num

        even_func = fun(times: Int, n: Nat128): (Int, Nat128) -> Int = <> with the full type written
            even = lambda <> we store the current lambda
            n2 = n / 2
            match n2
            to 1 | 2 | 4 => times
            to if is_even n2 => lambda(times + 1, n2)
            to if is_odd n2 => (
                fun(times2, nn) :-> Int =
                    n3 = 3 * nn + 1
                    match n3
                    to 1 | 2 | 4 => times2
                    to if is_even n3 => even(times2 + 1, n3)
                    to if is_odd n3 => lambda(times2 + 1, n3)
                )(times + 1, n2) <> we need to use parentheses to wrap the lambda and call it right after its definition
        if (is_even num) even_func(0, num) else even_func(-1, 2 * num) <> last expression returned, this if expression in this case

    <> Now, we will see how to write types in functions (it is the same for hard lambdas, just consider fun the name of the function)

    ::: you can do it like this, taking on account the thin short arrows are associative by the right
    def g1: Int -> Int -> Int <> is the same as def g1: Int -> (Int -> Int)
    g1 x = fun y = x + y
    println(g1 int1 int2)

    ::: this is the type of the function, not of the argument
    g2 x : Int -> (Int, Int) = (x, x ** 2)

    ::: now, the type of the argument
    g3 (x: Int) = (x, x ** 2)

    ::: all!
    g4 (x: Int) : Int -> (Int, Int) = (x, x - 12234)

    ::: you can write the return type and leave the argument one for the inference
    g5 x :-> Int = 22 * x

    ::: when you write a function, it is pattern matching, and the type is the one of the function (you need parentheses to write the type of arguments)
    g6 [a, b: Int, [c, d]: [Int, Int]] : [Int, Int, [Int, Int]] -> Int <> usually, the type is not required
    = a + b + c + d <> although not recommended, you can write the = symbol below

    ::: as said, if there are no parenthesis, the type is the one of the function...
    g7 x : Int -> Int = x * 5

    ::: ...while here we can see the type of the argument
    g8(x: Int): -> Int -> Int =
        <> remember: in lambdas, it works the same way
        fun y : Int -> Int = x * y

    ::: for lambdas with auto arguments, the type is the one of the lambda as well
    g9 =
        fun : Int -> Int = $0 + 3

    <> Not finished yet!
)
